The design of cache eviction policies has been a long-standing research topic, with a rich history of both heuristic and learning-based approaches.

Heuristics rely on fixed rules to evict objects. Traditional heuristic methods depend on a single observable feature. For example, LRU, LFU, Size, and FIFO policies prioritize eviction based on recency, frequency, size, and arrival time, respectively. Building on this, various abstract heuristics attempt to improve performance by combining multiple features. LRU-K \\cite{oneilLRUK1993} uses an LRU queue strictly for objects with $K$ or more accesses. 2Q \\cite{johnson2Q1994} adds a FIFO queue before the main LRU queue to filter out one-time accesses. GDSF \\cite{cherkasovaGDSF1998a} combines aged frequency and size into a single scoring mechanism. LRFU \\cite{dongheeleeLRFU2001} uniquely unifies LRU and LFU by exponentially decaying historical access frequencies. ARC \\cite{megiddoARC2003} dynamically balances between recency and frequency by adjusting the relative capacity of its LRU and LFU queues. wTinyLFU \\cite{einzigerTinyLFU2017} adopts a space-efficient frequency sketch to actively filter out one-time accesses before they even enter the main cache space. Furthermore, sophisticated heuristics define abstract metrics to capture complex workload patterns. LIRS \\cite{jiangLIRS2002} distinguishes cold and hot objects via inter-reference intervals. Hyperbolic \\cite{blanksteinHyperbolic2017} computes a priority score by dividing the access frequency by the elapsed time since the first access. LHD \\cite{beckmannLHD2018} defines the expected cache hit count per unit size as the hit density metric. Recently, hardware-efficient logic has regained popularity through methods like SIEVE \\cite{zhangSIEVE2024} and S3-FIFO \\cite{yangs3FIFO2023}, which deploy strict quick demotion and lazy promotion rules on simple FIFO structures. As a whole, heuristics are extremely lightweight and execute quickly upon cache misses. However, their static assumption inherently causes them to be highly environment-specific, leading to severe performance collapse when sudden workload pattern shifts occur.

Learning-based schemes have been proposed specifically to resolve the adaptability issues of traditional heuristics. However, directly inserting learning models into the cache miss path introduces a harsh tradeoff between model capability and inference latency.

\\textbf{Complex Model-based Policies:} To achieve maximum predictive scale and flexibility, some approaches deploy heavy architectures. Raven \\cite{huRaven2022} constructs a density estimation network built on Gaussian mixture models to accurately predict the reuse distance distribution of individual objects. Parrot \\cite{liuImitation2020} extensively modifies transformers and BiDAF network architectures to closely imitate Belady's optimal theoretical algorithm. Deep reinforcement learning has also been attempted, such as LRU-BaSE \\cite{wangLRUBase2024}, which applies a continuous DRL agent explicitly responsible for selecting the proper eviction object among the tail portion of an LRU list. While these complex models offer profound fitting capabilities, they inherently suffer from excessive computational overhead, leading to slow per-miss inference that renders them prohibitive in high-concurrence real-world systems.

\\textbf{Lightweight Model-based Policies:} To mitigate inference bottlenecks and allow real-time operational safety, recent mechanisms significantly sacrifice expressive capability. LRB \\cite{songLRB2020} trains Gradient Boosting Machines (GBM) to predict the reuse distance of objects online, selectively evicting the item with the worst prediction. 3LCACHE \\cite{zhou3Lcache2025} later extends LRB by aggressively lowering the online training and inference overheads through adaptive probability sampling. HALP \\cite{songHALP2023} shifts the paradigm by combining heuristics and ML: an MLP solely ranks and compares the four tail-end objects residing in an active LRU queue. GL-Cache \\cite{yangGLcache2023} clusters objects based on recent access time and uses GBM models to train and forecast utilization exclusively at the aggregate group level instead of per-object tracking. Finally, LHR \\cite{yanLHR2021} proposes the Hazard Rate criteria—the probability of an object being requested again inside a dynamic time window—and systematically targets the object holding the lowest hazard score for eviction. Alternatively, instead of learning object scores, methods like LeCaR \\cite{vietrLeCaR2018} and CACHEUS \\cite{rodriguezCACHEUS2021} frame the problem using regret minimization algorithms, dynamically alternating the operational weight between a handful of distinct heuristic components.

\\textbf{Offline Belady Imitation and OOD Issues:} Setting prediction limits aside, offline learning attempts have emerged through heavy historical emulation. Hawkeye \\cite{jainHawkeye2016} retrospectively tracks Belady simulations out-of-core on past access streams to construct historical occupancy vectors, mapping objects into positive hits or negative misses. Similarly, LFO \\cite{bergerLFO2018} discovers near-OPT sequences by strictly formulating best-case caching as an analytical min-cost flow problem over historical snapshots. Although computationally invisible on the critical path, these offline-trained imitation approaches routinely fail to resolve out-of-distribution (OOD) shift barriers. Because they inherently require pre-configuration based on historical assumptions, they fall short of fulfilling modern "plug-and-play" requirements for dynamically migrating and unprecedented CDN settings.
