\\textbf{Offline Belady Imitation and OOD Issues:} Setting prediction limits aside, offline learning attempts have emerged through heavy historical emulation. Hawkeye \\cite{jainHawkeye2016} retrospectively tracks Belady simulations out-of-core on past access streams to construct historical occupancy vectors, mapping objects into positive hits or negative misses. Similarly, LFO \\cite{bergerLFO2018} computes OPT decisions by formulating optimal caching as a min-cost flow problem on past access histories. Although computationally invisible on the critical path, these offline-trained imitation approaches routinely fail to resolve out-of-distribution (OOD) shift barriers. Because they are inherently trained offline and require pre-configuration based on historical assumptions, they fall short of fulfilling modern "plug-and-play" requirements for dynamically migrating and unprecedented CDN workloads.

